WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.412
A checkout is a chain: get the FX rate, convert, round to cents, charge.

2
00:00:07.412 --> 00:00:11.076
Each stage runs when the one before it hands it a value.

3
00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:13.671
Now the FX call fails with a 502.

4
00:00:13.671 --> 00:00:16.113
Which of the next three steps run?

5
00:00:18.333 --> 00:00:18.671
None.

6
00:00:18.671 --> 00:00:25.771
The failure skips every thenApply: their functions never run, and their futures fail with the same cause.

7
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.915
It slides down to the handlers.

8
00:00:27.915 --> 00:00:30.510
whenComplete only looks, and passes it on.

9
00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:34.959
exceptionally catches it, but this late, all it can return is: declined.

10
00:00:35.867 --> 00:00:38.939
Move exceptionally up, right after the FX call.

11
00:00:38.939 --> 00:00:44.627
Now it returns the last known rate, and every later step runs as if nothing went wrong.

12
00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:48.038
A future is only a box for the answer.

13
00:00:48.038 --> 00:00:50.989
The work runs somewhere else, on an io thread.

14
00:00:51.200 --> 00:01:01.852
With orTimeout of 200 ms, a timer fills the box with a TimeoutException at 200 ms, and it travels down the chain like any failure.

15
00:01:02.067 --> 00:01:04.230
The call never hears about it.

16
00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:09.205
It keeps its thread until 600 ms, and then its answer is thrown away.

17
00:01:09.433 --> 00:01:11.882
A timeout ends the wait, not the work.

18
00:01:11.882 --> 00:01:14.396
So give the client its own timeout too.

19
00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:20.192
Now ask three FX providers at once, and wait for all three with allOf.

20
00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.580
flaky fails at 50 ms.

21
00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:26.109
alpha answers at 100, beta at 300.

22
00:01:26.333 --> 00:01:31.014
allOf waits for all three, then fails, because one failed.

23
00:01:31.014 --> 00:01:35.776
One bad answer at 50 ms fails the whole call, 300 ms later.

24
00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:39.378
The fix: put a handle on each call first.

25
00:01:39.378 --> 00:01:43.753
It turns a failure into an empty answer, so allOf can only succeed.

26
00:01:43.967 --> 00:01:52.272
Now flaky's failure comes out of its handle as an empty answer, and at 300 ms the next step picks the lowest price.

27
00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:58.757
Give each call its own orTimeout too, so a provider that hangs becomes one more empty answer.

28
00:01:59.767 --> 00:02:04.147
In Java or Kotlin, each call gets its deadline, then its handle.

29
00:02:04.967 --> 00:02:05.548
The rule.

30
00:02:05.548 --> 00:02:09.099
A failure skips every stage until a handler catches it.

31
00:02:09.099 --> 00:02:12.069
Catch it where you still have a useful answer.

32
00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:13.358
Check yourself.

33
00:02:13.358 --> 00:02:19.281
The first stage fails, then comes thenApply A, then exceptionally, then thenApply B.

34
00:02:19.281 --> 00:02:20.832
Which of A and B runs?

35
00:02:23.533 --> 00:02:24.043
Only B.

36
00:02:24.043 --> 00:02:27.536
A is skipped, and exceptionally hands B a value.
